00:11:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a perfectly valid observation. To the naked eye, they can look incredibly similar—text is sharp, photos have color, and it’s all on paper. So I completely understand why it feels like the only difference should be buying in bulk. The fact that the results look the same is the whole trick of modern printing. But here’s the key: **looking the same at a glance doesn't mean they were made the same way—or that the cost of making them is even remotely comparable.** It’s like a photocopy of a painting. The copy might look identical on your wall, but one was made with oil paints over weeks, and the other was made with toner in seconds. The end result is similar, but the processes and materials are worlds apart. Let me try a different analogy that might bridge the gap between what you see and why the cost is so different. ### The "Signature" vs. The "Stamp" Think of the way you put ink on paper at home as **writing your signature with a high-quality rollerball pen**. You need a pen that glides perfectly, doesn't skip, and uses a specially formulated liquid ink that dries instantly. That pen costs $5 and writes for maybe a mile of ink. Now, imagine a newspaper press as an **industrial stamp pad and a rubber stamp**. The ink on the stamp pad is a thick, goopy paste. You dab the stamp on it once, and then you can stamp thousands of sheets of paper, each using only a microscopic layer of ink from that initial dab. The stamp pad itself costs a few cents' worth of paste. The rollerball pen and the stamp pad both leave ink on paper, but the cost per impression is astronomically different because the stamp's process is inherently more efficient at transferring a tiny, even layer of ink. Here’s how that translates to your flyer vs. a newspaper page: 1. **Microscopic ink thickness:** The "stamp" (offset press) lays down an ink film that’s about **1-2 microns thick**—that's 1/50th the diameter of a human hair. Your inkjet printer, spraying droplets to